tio":0.73,"watchRatio":0.5137},{"ratio":0.74,"watchRatio":0.5098},{"ratio":0.75,"watchRatio":0.5084500000000001},{"ratio":0.76,"watchRatio":0.5057},{"ratio":0.77,"watchRatio":0.50285},{"ratio":0.78,"watchRatio":0.49795},{"ratio":0.79,"watchRatio":0.49760000000000004},{"ratio":0.8,"watchRatio":0.4964},{"ratio":0.81,"watchRatio":0.492},{"ratio":0.82,"watchRatio":0.489},{"ratio":0.83,"watchRatio":0.48634999999999995},{"ratio":0.84,"watchRatio":0.48395000000000005},{"ratio":0.85,"watchRatio":0.48169999999999996},{"ratio":0.86,"watchRatio":0.47975},{"ratio":0.87,"watchRatio":0.47440000000000004},{"ratio":0.88,"watchRatio":0.46885},{"ratio":0.89,"watchRatio":0.4655},{"ratio":0.9,"watchRatio":0.4637},{"ratio":0.91,"watchRatio":0.45840000000000003},{"ratio":0.92,"watchRatio":0.45345},{"ratio":0.93,"watchRatio":0.4488},{"ratio":0.94,"watchRatio":0.4458},{"ratio":0.95,"watchRatio":0.43979999999999997},{"ratio":0.96,"watchRatio":0.43235},{"ratio":0.97,"watchRatio":0.4235},{"ratio":0.98,"watchRatio":0.4112},{"ratio":0.99,"watchRatio":0.39765},{"ratio":1,"watchRatio":0.39415}],"script":{"source_path":"all-146-tagged.json","words":260,"wpm":null,"lang":"en","chapters":[],"blocks":[],"text":"Could fascism and communism ever truly be synthesized? Subhas Chandra Bose thought so, and he didn't mean Germany or Russia. He meant independent India. Bose was the odd man out in Congress. While others preached moderation, he preached that freedom would have to be taken by force. And for that, he developed his own ideology, Samyavada, or the doctrine of synthesis. In 1935, he put it in writing, and I quote, \"The next phase in world history will produce a synthesis between communism and fascism.\" And he meant every word. His India would have one all-powerful state, no liberal democracy, complete suppression of dissent, [music] and the entire country industrially rebuilt on state command. Gandhi's vote socialism was too soft, according to him, and Nehru's Fabian socialism too slow. His third path made him enemies on every side. But here's the craziest part. His reason for rejecting communism sounds nothing like a typical leftist. It was [music] anti-national and anti-religious, he said. It could never work in India. Remember, this is the same Bose who said that India's, quote, \"most important cementing factor has been the Hindu religion.\" The communists branded him a fascist. The British hunted him as a revolutionary. Nobody was ever really able to place him, either then or now. So, what was [music] he? Neither. Bose was loyal to exactly one ideal, India's freedom, and he would borrow from heaven or hell to achieve it. He never lived to see the India he imagined, but the army that he raised shook the empire to its core."}}